==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/nɒk/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-nock.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/nɑk/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-knock.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒk|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|knock}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Arrow-Selfnock.jpeg|thumb|nock ({{senseno|en|arrow}})]]
From {{inh|en|enm|nokke}}, attested since the 14th century, probably from a Scandinavian/{{der|en|gmq|-}} language (compare {{cog|sv|nock||notch}}, but compare {{der|en|nl|nok}}, from {{der|en|dum|nocke||tip, point}}.  Both could be related to {{m|en|nook||corner, recess}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# Either of the two grooves in a [[bow]] that hold the [[bowstring]].
#* {{RQ:Doyle White Company|passage=Meanwhile there had been bustle and preparation in all parts of the great vessel. The archers stood in groups about the decks, new-stringing their bows, and testing that they were firm at the '''nocks'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Sir Nigel|passage=Each drew his string from its waterproof case and bent the huge arc of his war-bow as he fitted it into the '''nocks'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|arrow}} {{lb|en|archery}} The [[notch]] at the rear of an [[arrow]] that fits on the bowstring.
#* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|year=1843|passage=He took his arrow by the '''nock'''.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The upper fore corner of a [[boom]] sail or [[trysail]].

